---
import Callout from "astro-theme-university/components/Callout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import CourseLayout from "../../layouts/CourseLayout.astro";
import EvidenceCode from "../../components/EvidenceCode.astro";
import FileList from "../../components/FileList.astro";
import Numbers from "../../components/Numbers.astro";
import SpecimenTable from "../../components/SpecimenTable.astro";
import { HELD_IMAGES, allEvidence } from "../../lib/evidence-manifest";
import { evidenceInfo, evidenceUrl, formatBytes } from "../../lib/evidence";

const totalBytes = allEvidence.reduce((sum, id) => sum + evidenceInfo(id).bytes, 0);
---

<CourseLayout
  title="Evidence and provenance"
  kicker="Reference"
  description="Where every number on this site comes from, what the corpus is, what it is
    not, and the two images this course refuses to publish."
>
  <p>
    This course teaches from one curated research package. Every claim on every
    week page resolves to a file in it, and every one of those files is
    published here byte-for-byte — not retyped, not tidied, not summarised. The
    snippets you see inside week pages are sliced out of these same files while
    the site builds, so a listing on a page and the artefact behind it cannot
    drift apart.
  </p>

  <Numbers
    items={[
      { value: String(allEvidence.length), label: "artefacts published verbatim, with their SHA-256 recorded", source: "artefacts/published-evidence.json" },
      { value: formatBytes(totalBytes), label: "of source, IR, disassembly, graphs, solver queries and result files", source: "sum of the published set" },
      { value: "21", label: "retained Windows x64 specimens, catalogued here and distributed through the lab", sourceId: "BINARY_INDEX.json" },
      { value: "0", label: "executables downloadable from this site — deliberately", source: "see the execution boundary below" },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>The frozen source</h2>
    <p>
      One 483-line C file underlies the entire corpus. It was frozen — hash
      recorded — before any build, and re-checked before every one of the
      sixty-eight builds in the comparison matrix. That is the fact that makes
      the rest of the course possible: no difference between two specimens can
      be a difference in the program.
    </p>
    <EvidenceCode
      id="assets/source/SHOWCASE_VERSION.txt"
      lang="text"
      title="SHOWCASE_VERSION.txt"
      note="the identity every build was checked against"
      class="sc-wide"
    />
    <p>
      The same hash is recorded independently in the matrix report, the
      BinProtect report and the corpus description, and the copy this site
      publishes hashes to{" "}
      <code>{evidenceInfo("assets/source/showcase.c").sha256.slice(0, 16)}…</code> —
      which is how you can tell nothing was edited in transit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its six exported targets each exist for a reason:
      <code>demo_substitution</code> has twenty-four repeated arithmetic sites,
      <code>demo_bogus_control_flow</code> has four genuine source conditions
      with memorable constants, <code>demo_flattening</code> has an
      if/else, a four-iteration loop and a four-way switch that reconverges, and
      <code>demo_calls</code>, <code>demo_data</code> and
      <code>demo_combined</code> cover calls, string and table data, and a mixed
      case. The program prints its six answers and checks them itself, which is
      what makes "did this build work?" a question with a one-line answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>The specimens</h2>
    <p>
      Twenty-one executables, all Windows x64 PE. The retained runtime column is
      inherited from the original experiments and was not re-run during
      curation; the lab column is the course's own rule about what you may
      execute.
    </p>
    <SpecimenTable />
    <p>
      One row is the reason the last column exists. The file named
      <code>INCORRECT_DEOBFUSCATION_DO_NOT_USE_AS_WORKING_BINARY.exe</code> is a
      structurally valid PE, produced by a real tool that exited zero, which
      returns the wrong answer. It is kept, under that name, as negative
      evidence. It is not a broken build to be fixed and it is not to be
      executed as a working program.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>The execution boundary</h2>
    <Callout type="warning">
      <p>
        <strong>No executable is downloadable from this site.</strong> Obfuscated
        Windows binaries do not belong on a public web page, one of these
        specimens must never be a click away from looking runnable, and a
        course site is not a distribution channel. The specimens live on the
        lab image; this page publishes the identity you need to confirm that the
        file in front of you is the right one.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>
      Reading works anywhere. Source, IR, generated C, exact disassembly,
      recovered graphs, solver queries and every result file are published here
      and are enough for every core exercise in the course, offline, on any
      platform. Execution and debugging need the Windows x64 lab image. No Wine
      route is supported and no Linux rebuild is equivalent — a rebuild would be
      a different specimen with a different ABI and different lowering.
    </p>
    <p>
      No BinProtect protected output is bundled either, and none is published
      here. All eight tested protected outputs failed at runtime — seven access
      violations and one hang — so that lane is static-analysis evidence only
      and has no runnable specimen to distribute.
    </p>
    <p>
      No PDB or MAP file is included anywhere, on purpose. Navigate by export
      name or by loaded base plus RVA. For a rewritten specimen, decline the
      original PDB if a debugger offers it: the RSDS identifier can survive
      patching while the instruction mappings inside the rewritten function go
      stale, which is one of the four traps week 12 collects.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>Two images on publication hold</h2>
    <p>
      Twenty-five disassembler screenshots were supplied with the package and
      all twenty-five were inspected. Two of them have visible content that
      contradicts their filename, and this site does not display either:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        HELD_IMAGES.map((name) => (
          <li>
            <code>{name}</code>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      The first is labelled as one obfuscator's rewrite; its visible right pane
      carries state constants and a branch target that match a different
      family's rewritten graph. The second is named for the arithmetic target
      and both of its visible pane titles say <code>demo_flattening</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Neither was renamed, deleted or quietly repurposed. They stay in the
      package with their holds recorded in{" "}
      <a href={evidenceUrl("VISUAL_INDEX.md")}>VISUAL_INDEX.md</a>, and this
      course uses the alternatives the index recommends instead. A filename is a
      claim about provenance like any other, and a course that teaches you to
      check the analyst's evidence should check its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is also evidence the package simply does not contain: no confirmed
      screenshot of the arithmetic target under source-level encoding, and no
      screenshot of any lifted IR. Week 5 and week 11 use the retained
      disassembly and the retained IR text instead of an illustration, and say
      so.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>Reading the artefacts</h2>
    <p>
      Three things about these files routinely confuse people on first contact,
      and none of them is a defect.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Two graph conventions.</strong> The branch-rewriting study used a
      dedicated PE-byte audit that does not split blocks at calls and treats
      <code>ret</code> as terminal. The unflattening study used Miasm's
      recursive graphs, which do split at calls. A block count from one is not
      comparable with a block count from the other, and every page that quotes
      one names it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Three disassembly flavours.</strong> The retained listings were
      dumped at different times with different settings. Some are AT&T order
      with a byte column, some are Intel order with a byte column, and some are
      Intel order with the bytes packed. Read the operand order before comparing
      two listings — it is the cheapest provenance lesson available.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Windows paths inside the files.</strong> Result files and reports
      carry absolute <code>E:\Workspace\…</code> paths from the machine the
      experiments ran on. They are provenance, not instructions, and the
      research tree they point at is deliberately not bundled.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sc-section">
    <h2>Start here</h2>
    <FileList
      items={[
        { id: "CLAIMS_AND_CAVEATS.md", what: "every result, its safe formulation and the overclaim beside it" },
        { id: "BEST_EVIDENCE_BY_TOPIC.md", what: "topic to artefact, with the matched comparator for each" },
        { id: "BINARY_GUIDE.md", what: "per-specimen runtime status, execution rule and export RVAs" },
        { id: "VISUAL_INDEX.md", what: "all 25 screenshots, what is visible in each, and the two holds" },
        { id: "EXPERIMENTAL_CORPUS.md", what: "why this program, these functions and these constants" },
        { id: "assets/reports/FINAL_RESEARCH_OVERVIEW.md", what: "the whole study in thirteen sections, including what did not generalise" },
        { id: "assets/tables/research/selected_metrics.md", what: "every headline number with its convention and provenance" },
        { id: "VALIDATION_REPORT.md", what: "the curation QA: 298 assets hash-checked, holds recorded, limits stated" },
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      The complete published set, with a SHA-256 for each entry, is at{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/artefacts/published-evidence.json")}>
        published-evidence.json
      </a>. The seventeen full research reports are under{" "}
      <code>/artefacts/reports/</code> and are linked from the week that
      uses them.
    </p>
  </section>
</CourseLayout>
